## Tutorials

❮ Scanner Methods

ThehasNextBoolean()method returnstrueif the next token represents a boolean value. A token represents a boolean value if its value matches one of the strings "true" or "false". The match is case-insensitive, which means that values like "True" and "FALSE" also represent a boolean value.

❮ Scanner Methods

### Code Examples

```
Example
Print the first boolean value that is found:
// Create a scanner object
Scanner myObj = new Scanner("The value is false");

// Skip tokens until a boolean is found
while (myObj.hasNext() && !myObj.hasNextBoolean()) {
  myObj.next();
}

// If there is a boolean then print it
if (myObj.hasNextBoolean()) {
  System.out.print("The boolean value is ");
  System.out.println(myObj.nextBoolean());
} else {
  System.out.println("No boolean found");
}
```

